\section{Reach a Constructive Reaction}

Esterel uses logical synchrony: concurrent statements react to the same input event set, exchange signals within the instant, and reach quiescence before logical time advances. A signal's present/absent status is global to the reaction. Sequential composition orders control within an instant, while \texttt{pause} crosses an instant boundary. This source semantics does not require physical zero-time execution; generated code must finish within the environment's timing budget \cite{berry1992esterel}.

Constructiveness is stronger than finding a classically consistent assignment of signal statuses. The compiler must be able to justify presence from emissions or absence from the impossibility of emission without circularly assuming the conclusion. Programs whose behavior depends on ``emit \(S\) iff \(S\) is absent'' have no constructive reaction even if a Boolean equation appears to admit reasoning by cases.

\section{Synchronize and Preempt With ABRO}

The classic Esterel pattern waits for both inputs, emits \texttt{O}, and restarts on \texttt{R}:

\begin{verbatim}
module ABRO:
  input A, B, R;
  output O;
  loop
    [ await A || await B ];
    emit O
  each R
end module
\end{verbatim}

The parallel waits remember arrivals across reactions. When both have completed, \texttt{O} is emitted in that logical reaction. The strong \texttt{each R} preemption restarts the body according to Esterel's specified priority; an implementation must not let host-thread scheduling choose whether an emission escapes. \Cref{fig:visual-364} sketches the reaction state.

